# the object's debug records as DWARF 5 debugging information
#
# one compile unit holds the records, written into .debug_info,
# .debug_abbrev, .debug_str, .debug_str_offsets, .debug_addr,
# .debug_loclists and .debug_rnglists, or the names the format's row gives
# them, in DWARF 5 forms throughout: a string by its index into the string
# offsets (DW_FORM_strx), an address by its index into the address table
# (DW_FORM_addrx), and a location or range list by its index
# (DW_FORM_loclistx, DW_FORM_rnglistx). every address is a datum of the
# address width relocated against the mark or symbol it names, and every
# reference between the sections an offset relocated by the section-relative
# kind against the start of the section it names. a string, an address and a
# list each take the next index the first time an entry names it, in the
# order the entries are written.
#
# the unit's entry comes first, then one entry per type in table order, then
# each global, then the abstract entry of every callee inlined, then each
# function with its variables and the code inlined in it. a type names
# another by its entry's offset, filled once every type is written. a
# tagged union is a structure of its discriminant and a variant part of its
# cases. a variable's location list has an entry per range where DWARF can
# state where it is, so a debugger reads it as optimised out elsewhere: a
# frame slot or a webassembly location with steps over it is one DWARF's
# expressions do not state. a global of the unit is at its symbol's address,
# a thread-local at its symbol's offset in its thread's block before
# DW_OP_form_tls_address in the form the format's row states, with no
# location where the object's relocations write none of the row's kinds, and
# one another unit defines is a declaration. a declaration's file and line,
# and the unit's DW_AT_stmt_list, are written where the object's line records
# lowered to a DWARF line table, its file k the line table's file k. the
# encoding is 32-bit DWARF.

use std.types.bool.bool;
use std.types.bool.false;
use std.types.bool.true;
use std.types.error.err;
use std.types.option.opt;
use std.types.result.res;
use std.types.size.usize;
use std.types.string.str;
use std.types.view.View;

use A: std.allocator;
use std.collections.vector;
use std.collections.vector.Vector;

